1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,743
Tactile Reflex System.

2
00:00:02,863 --> 00:00:07,576
A deterministic, FPGA-based safety reflex for robotic gripping.

3
00:00:07,696 --> 00:00:12,992
When a gripper squeezes too hard, the decision to stop must not wait for software.

4
00:00:13,112 --> 00:00:18,154
Everything in this video is simulation. No physical hardware was tested.

5
00:00:20,067 --> 00:00:26,492
A gripper has two jobs: understand what it is touching, and react at once when the force is too high.

6
00:00:26,612 --> 00:00:33,508
Software control loops add latency that varies: scheduling, communication, and inference time.

7
00:00:33,628 --> 00:00:46,299
Here, the decision tree runs on a laptop. On the development host its walk averages 11.6 microseconds and peaked at 96.5, before any serial link delay.

8
00:00:46,419 --> 00:00:51,888
So the work is split. The AI advises. Fixed hardware logic protects.

9
00:00:52,008 --> 00:00:57,721
The AI can only lower the command, never raise it, and the hard limits ignore it completely.

10
00:00:59,633 --> 00:01:05,400
A force sensor feeds a 12-bit AD7476A converter over SPI.

11
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:12,161
The ADC interface reads one 16-bit frame: four leading zeros, then twelve data bits.

12
00:01:12,281 --> 00:01:16,623
Input conditioning registers the sample and flags invalid readings.

13
00:01:16,743 --> 00:01:23,276
A fixed 256-entry lookup table turns force into a PWM duty command.

14
00:01:23,396 --> 00:01:27,701
The safety governor enforces the limits and can force a safe state.

15
00:01:27,821 --> 00:01:32,896
The PWM generator drives the DRV8871 motor driver.

16
00:01:33,016 --> 00:01:37,237
A fault handler and the emergency stop feed the governor directly.

17
00:01:37,357 --> 00:01:43,364
The advisory AI path arrives over UART, is integrity checked, and can only lower the command.

18
00:01:43,484 --> 00:01:53,554
The target is a Terasic DE2-115 board at 50 megahertz. This is the prototype design; no physical validation is part of this work.

19
00:01:55,467 --> 00:02:08,909
In the ADC interface, a counter makes the SPI clock, samples on the falling edge, and shifts in sixteen bits. One frame takes 104 clock cycles: 2.08 microseconds.

20
00:02:09,029 --> 00:02:14,377
A reading with a frame error, or at or above hex F F 0, is flagged invalid.

21
00:02:14,497 --> 00:02:26,704
The lookup table indexes on the top eight bits of the force code. No contact commands 180. The command ramps down to a hold level of 60, then to zero at the force limit.

22
00:02:26,824 --> 00:02:39,429
The governor ORs every trip cause: force over limit, sensor fault, emergency stop, invalid reading, not yet armed, and predicted over-force. Any one forces the command to zero.

23
00:02:39,549 --> 00:02:49,805
The PWM generator is a 12-bit counter: 12.2 kilohertz at 50 megahertz. The kill input forces the output low on the next clock.

24
00:02:51,733 --> 00:03:05,610
First, the force limit. The AI claims a normal grip, but force jumps above the limit of 2560 counts. The governor enters the safe state, PWM goes low, and the AI is overridden.

25
00:03:05,730 --> 00:03:21,996
Second, predictive trip. On a fast ramp the governor watches the rise per three millisecond window. In the RTL replay it tripped at a force code of 2015, twelve steps before the limit was crossed at 2569.

26
00:03:22,116 --> 00:03:31,768
The price is occasional early trips: on the simulated test episodes, 6.5 percent of episodes that never reached the limit still tripped early.

27
00:03:31,888 --> 00:03:45,993
Third, hysteresis. After a trip the output stays inhibited until force falls below 2304. A force of 2304 still holds. 2303 releases.

28
00:03:46,113 --> 00:03:58,066
Fourth, the emergency stop. Safe state rises in four clock cycles and PWM is low in five: 100 nanoseconds. It stays latched until an operator clears it.

29
00:03:58,186 --> 00:04:11,621
Fifth, invalid sensor input. One corrupted ADC frame inhibits the drive for exactly one frame and latches nothing. Four consecutive bad frames latch a sensor fault until an operator clears it.

30
00:04:11,741 --> 00:04:19,365
If samples stop arriving, a watchdog raises the fault at 2000 clock cycles, which is 40 microseconds.

31
00:04:19,485 --> 00:04:27,519
In every case the outcome is the same: PWM low, safe state asserted, and no recovery without the right conditions.

32
00:04:29,433 --> 00:04:37,997
Now the evidence. All 175 directed checks across 12 testbenches pass in the existing RTL regression.

33
00:04:38,117 --> 00:04:50,575
One thousand eight hundred test vectors were replayed through the RTL twice: once with the real decision tree, once with an AI that always claims normal grip. Both gave zero safe-state violations.

34
00:04:50,695 --> 00:05:16,568
A separate experiment ran 15 scenarios and 159 checks. A software model met all 159. The RTL met 157; the two misses come from a stimulus that lasted about 240 ADC frames instead of one. A threshold-only baseline met 93, by design, because it has no hysteresis, fault, or stop handling.

35
00:05:16,688 --> 00:05:23,813
On a noisy crossing near the limit, the baseline toggled seven times. The hysteresis design inhibited once.

36
00:05:23,933 --> 00:05:38,160
In simulation, a valid sample reaches PWM low in four clock cycles, 80 nanoseconds. An input change takes 105 to 192 cycles, mostly waiting for the next ADC frame.

37
00:05:38,280 --> 00:05:45,710
Frame by frame, 577 comparisons between the model and the RTL showed no differences.

38
00:05:45,830 --> 00:05:54,846
The decision tree reaches 91.9 percent accuracy on the simulated dataset, against 85.4 for a force-only baseline.

39
00:05:54,966 --> 00:06:10,965
What this does not establish: physical safety. Inputs are synthetic, the ADC is a behavioural model, the AI dataset is simulated, 13 step-level model differences remain explained but not removed, and the Wokwi demonstration has not been run.

40
00:06:12,867 --> 00:06:16,609
Putting it together. A valid ADC sample arrives.

41
00:06:16,729 --> 00:06:22,193
One clock cycle each: conditioning, lookup, governor, and PWM.

42
00:06:22,313 --> 00:06:29,920
Four cycles later, if any limit is exceeded, the output is low. The AI can advise, but it cannot override.

43
00:06:31,833 --> 00:06:33,776
Tactile Reflex System.

44
00:06:33,896 --> 00:06:46,752
An advisory-only AI, checked with an always-wrong AI. A slope-predictive pre-limit in plain hardware. A self-checking AI link. An instant PWM kill with a latched, fail-safe stop.

45
00:06:46,872 --> 00:06:51,121
These are design-level descriptions, not prior-art conclusions.

46
00:06:51,241 --> 00:07:00,357
Proposed next steps, not done: Quartus synthesis and timing, board bring-up, recorded sensor data, and the Wokwi run.
